Here is the commit message:
Call AbstractElement getID() insteado of getId()

Relying on capitalization significance to differentiate methods on
this object seems like asking for trouble.  For now, just fix the
capitalization bug, but we might want to
refactor getID() and getId() to more distinct names.

Here is the root cause:
The `equals()` method in `AbstractElement` was designed to compare Titan vertices/edges by their internal unique identifiers, but due to the presence of two case-sensitive, similarly-named methods—`getId()` (likely inherited from the Blueprints `Element` interface, returning an `Object`) and `getID()` (Titan's internal method returning a primitive `long`)—the developer mistakenly called `getId()` instead of `getID()`. Since Java treats these as entirely distinct methods, the wrong one was invoked. This could cause incorrect equality results if `getId()` does not reliably return the canonical internal ID (e.g., due to differences in type, boxing, or implementation inconsistencies across element types), rather than using the guaranteed-correct internal `getID()` method with primitive `long` comparison via `==`. The ambiguity between two near-identical method names on the same class made this mistake easy to introduce and hard to detect at compile time.

Here is the patch:

     @Override
     public boolean equals(Object other) {
         if (this==other) {
             return true;
         } else if (other==null) {
             return false;
         } else if (this instanceof TitanVertex && other instanceof TitanVertex) {
-            return getId().equals(((TitanVertex)other).getId());
+            return getID() == ((TitanVertex)other).getID();
         } else if (this instanceof TitanEdge && other instanceof TitanEdge) {
-            return getId().equals(((TitanEdge)other).getId());
+            return getID() == ((TitanEdge)other).getID();
         } else {
             return false;
         }
     }



